WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.130
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.700
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.425
Unit sixteen: Ethics of AI. Today you will assign responsibility using passive modals.

4
00:00:15.725 --> 00:00:18.343
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.428
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:24.877
Algorithmic bias.

7
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:31.633
Algorithmic bias means unfair patterns in an algorithm's results, often reflecting biased data.

8
00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:35.702
Algorithmic bias denied qualified applicants interviews.

9
00:00:36.317 --> 00:00:39.631
Your turn. Say: Algorithmic bias.

10
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:44.153
Algorithmic bias.

11
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:46.301
Accountability.

12
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:52.172
Accountability means the obligation to explain and answer for one's actions.

13
00:00:52.783 --> 00:00:56.373
Accountability requires that someone can be called to answer.

14
00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:59.879
Your turn. Say: Accountability.

15
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:04.106
Accountability.

16
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.292
Transparency.

17
00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:11.709
Transparency means openness about how a system works and makes decisions.

18
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:16.069
Transparency allows outsiders to audit automated decisions.

19
00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:19.719
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

20
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:23.812
Transparency.

21
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:25.795
Autonomy.

22
00:01:26.783 --> 00:01:31.349
Autonomy means the ability to act independently, without outside control.

23
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.029
Greater machine autonomy complicates the question of blame.

24
00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:38.176
Your turn. Say: Autonomy.

25
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:42.127
Autonomy.

26
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:44.337
AI alignment.

27
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:50.217
AI alignment means making AI systems pursue goals consistent with human values.

28
00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:53.710
AI alignment is a central research challenge.

29
00:01:54.317 --> 00:01:57.468
Your turn. Say: AI alignment.

30
00:02:00.333 --> 00:02:02.391
AI alignment.

31
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.523
Moral agency.

32
00:02:05.517 --> 00:02:10.477
Moral agency means the capacity to make moral choices and be held responsible.

33
00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:14.604
Most thinkers deny that current software has moral agency.

34
00:02:15.217 --> 00:02:18.403
Your turn. Say: Moral agency.

35
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:22.871
Moral agency.

36
00:02:23.617 --> 00:02:24.711
Regulation.

37
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:29.469
Regulation means official rules that govern how something may be done.

38
00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:33.871
Regulation of high-risk systems is advancing in several regions.

39
00:02:34.483 --> 00:02:37.240
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

40
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.124
Regulation.

41
00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:43.418
Societal impact.

42
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:48.471
Societal impact means the effect of something on society as a whole.

43
00:02:49.083 --> 00:02:52.139
A societal impact assessment preceded the rollout.

44
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.157
Your turn. Say: Societal impact.

45
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.651
Societal impact.

46
00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:05.094
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:11.215
Visal, a hiring algorithm rejected many qualified women. Who is to blame, the program?

48
00:03:12.317 --> 00:03:19.915
Not the program, since software lacks moral agency. Developers should be held accountable for algorithmic bias.

49
00:03:21.017 --> 00:03:22.830
Even if they didn't intend it?

50
00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:32.432
Intent isn't the standard. Systems that affect lives must be tested before deployment, and the results ought to be published.

51
00:03:33.517 --> 00:03:34.924
And the company using it?

52
00:03:36.017 --> 00:03:44.335
It should be held responsible too. Regulation could be introduced so that both parties are required to demonstrate fairness.

53
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.861
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.717 --> 00:03:50.831
Grammar focus: Passive modal (should be held).

55
00:03:51.131 --> 00:03:52.318
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:53.017 --> 00:03:56.142
Developers should be held accountable for algorithmic bias.

57
00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.502
Systems must be tested before deployment.

58
00:04:00.417 --> 00:04:03.357
The results ought to be published for public scrutiny.

59
00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:13.140
Passive modals: should be held, must be tested, ought to be published, could have been prevented. After 'should' and 'must' there is no 'to'; after 'ought' there is.

60
00:04:13.883 --> 00:04:17.755
Be careful! Don't say: Developers should hold accountable.

61
00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.501
Say: Developers should be held accountable.

62
00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:24.800
should be + past participle.

63
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.286
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.798
Who Answers for the Machine?.

65
00:04:32.098 --> 00:04:39.589
As algorithms increasingly decide who receives loans, jobs or parole, the question of responsibility has become urgent.

66
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:48.834
Because current systems lack moral agency, most ethicists argue that blame must fall on the humans who design, deploy and profit from them.

67
00:04:49.833 --> 00:04:57.928
Algorithmic bias, which often reflects prejudice hidden in training data, should be detected through audits and corrected before systems are released.

68
00:04:58.933 --> 00:05:03.754
Transparency is essential, for decisions that cannot be explained cannot be challenged.

69
00:05:04.767 --> 00:05:12.374
Looking further ahead, AI alignment asks whether increasingly autonomous systems can be made to pursue goals that humans would endorse.

70
00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:17.306
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.800
Developers should blank accountable for bias.

72
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.474
A, hold. B, be held. C, be holding.

73
00:05:28.033 --> 00:05:29.870
The answer is B: be held.

74
00:05:31.017 --> 00:05:34.026
The obligation to answer for one's actions is blank.

75
00:05:34.326 --> 00:05:38.200
A, autonomy. B, accountability. C, alignment.

76
00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:44.370
The answer is B: accountability.

77
00:05:45.517 --> 00:05:48.503
Unfair patterns in an algorithm's results are blank.

78
00:05:48.803 --> 00:05:53.559
A, algorithmic bias. B, transparency. C, regulation.

79
00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.390
The answer is A: algorithmic bias.

80
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.306
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:02.606 --> 00:06:09.696
A, Systems must be test first.. B, Systems must tested first.. C, Systems must be tested first..

82
00:06:13.233 --> 00:06:16.545
The answer is C: Systems must be tested first..

83
00:06:17.683 --> 00:06:20.147
According to the reading, blame must fall on…

84
00:06:20.447 --> 00:06:25.656
A, the software itself. B, the humans who design and deploy it. C, no one.

85
00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.604
The answer is B: the humans who design and deploy it.

86
00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.673
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:37.517 --> 00:06:41.536
Who should be held accountable when an AI system causes harm?

88
00:06:58.350 --> 00:06:59.734
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:00.034 --> 00:07:28.512
The humans and organisations behind the system should be held accountable, because software cannot bear moral responsibility. Developers must test for bias, and companies that deploy systems should be required to monitor and correct them. Where harm is serious, regulation should assign liability clearly, so that victims are not left without recourse. Accountability works best when it is shared and enforceable.

90
00:07:28.812 --> 00:07:30.475
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:31.383 --> 00:07:36.044
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive modal (should be held).

92
00:07:36.344 --> 00:07:41.574
Today's value is ethical responsibility. Take ownership of the effects of what you create.

93
00:07:41.874 --> 00:07:44.608
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:44.908 --> 00:07:47.757
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

